"The opening verses of the surah were revealed concerning the situation of Khawlah bint Tha'labah, whose husband Aws ibn Samit had pronounced zihar upon her. Zihar is a man's saying to his wife, \"You are to me like the back of my mother.\" In the pre-Islamic period of ignorance (Jahiliyyah), this statement was considered a cause of definitive separation. Since no ruling had been revealed on this matter, the Prophet (peace be upon him) followed custom and told Khawlah that she had become unlawful to her husband. However, Khawlah persistently presented her grievance to Allah, and eventually the verses were revealed. Allah hearing her voice and answering her plea demonstrates that He hears all things and is responsive to the problems of His servants.\n\nIn Islam, zihar is described as an abominable and false statement. This is because comparing a woman to one's mother does not reflect reality and injures the woman's dignity. However, when this statement is uttered, it does not remain devoid of legal consequence; it leads to a state of prohibition. To make amends for this sin, Allah has prescribed the path of expiation (kaffarah). Expiation, for a man who wishes to revoke the zihar before touching his wife, consists in this order: freeing a slave; if he lacks the means for this, fasting for two consecutive months; and if he is unable to do this, feeding sixty poor people. These three options are ordered according to the individual's means. While the Hanafis do not stipulate that the slave to be freed must be a believer, the Shafi'is have made being a believer a condition.\n\nThese rulings are the limits ordained by Allah. Whoever transgresses these limits and opposes Allah and His Messenger will suffer disgrace in this world and be subjected to a humiliating punishment in the Hereafter. At this point, the verse both warns the believers and threatens the disbelievers. It is emphasized that Allah knows all things and takes count of everything that is done, one by one.\n\nStarting from the seventh verse, the topic of secret counsels (najwa) is addressed. Allah knows whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth; there are no three persons in secret counsel but He is their fourth, nor five but He is their sixth. No matter how few or how many they may be, Allah is with them at all times. This verse was revealed upon the polytheists' question, \"Does Allah know what we talk about?\" Furthermore, it also points to the situation where Companions engaged in private conversation with the Prophet (peace be upon him).\n\nIn the eighth verse, those who secretly conspire in sin, aggression, and disobedience to the Messenger—despite having been forbidden from doing so—are condemned. The Jews and the hypocrites are criticized for greeting the Prophet with phrases such as \"Death be upon you.\" Their conduct is an insolence that invites the punishment of Allah. In the ninth verse, the believers are commanded to make righteousness and piety (taqwa) the foundation of their private conversations, and to avoid sin and aggression. The tenth verse states that such secret counsels stem from Satan and are intended to cause grief to the believers, yet Satan cannot harm them in any way except by Allah's leave.\n\nThe eleventh verse concerns making room for one another in gatherings and showing respect to those possessed of knowledge. In the twelfth and thirteenth verses, it was commanded that those who wished to consult the Prophet privately should offer a charity beforehand; later, this ruling was eased and pardoned. The believers are commanded to establish prayer, give zakah, and obey. All these verses aim at organizing social life and purifying hearts from evils.\n\nAccording to the narrations concerning the occasions of revelation of the verses, when the Jews greeted the Prophet (peace be upon him), they used to say, \"Death be upon you.\" Our Prophet (peace be upon him) would respond to them, \"And upon you.\" Aisha became very angry at this and cursed them, but our Prophet (peace be upon him) cautioned her. The Jews would say, \"Why does not Allah punish us for what we say?\" It was upon this behavior of theirs that this verse was revealed. The punishment of Hell is sufficient for them; what a wretched destination it is!\n\nAddressing the believers, it was made known that secret counsels (najwa) are not forbidden entirely, but that they must not be regarding sin, aggression, and disobedience to the Prophet. Private conversations must be based upon goodness, righteousness, and piety (taqwa). Furthermore, when three people are together, two whispering to one another may cause grief to the other; this has been forbidden. Likewise, speaking in a language that the person next to them does not understand is not permissible if it saddens them.\n\nIn the verse concerning making room in gatherings, the believers were commanded to make space when told to \"spread out.\" According to a narration, when a group from among the veterans of Badr arrived, they were not given room in the assembly; this distressed the Prophet (peace be upon him), and he had some individuals stand up. When the hypocrites criticized this, this verse was revealed. In addition, when told to rise or move up, they were commanded to rise promptly; it was declared that Allah will raise those who believe and those who have been given knowledge in degrees. This verse is evidence for the virtue of knowledge. In the hadiths, it is emphasized that scholars are superior to worshippers and that knowledge is the inheritance of the prophets. However, those who do not act upon their knowledge are condemned.\n\nThose who wished to consult privately with the Prophet were commanded to give charity before their consultation. This was to demonstrate sincerity and to bring joy to the poor. However, this ruling was abrogated (naskh) shortly thereafter. Ali stated that he was the first and the last person to act upon this verse. Subsequently, Allah granted permission to consult the Prophet without giving charity, while prayer, zakah, and obedience were commanded.\n\nThe hypocrites who take as allies a people with whom Allah is angry (the Jews) are condemned. They belong neither to the believers nor to the Jews. They knowingly swear false oaths, taking their oaths as a shield to turn people away from the path of Allah. For them is a humiliating punishment. Satan has overtaken them and made them forget the remembrance of Allah. They are the party of Satan, and they are the losers. Those who oppose Allah and His Messenger are among the most abased. Allah has decreed: \"I and My messengers shall surely prevail.\"\n\nThose who believe in Allah and the Last Day do not take as allies those who oppose Allah and His Messenger, even if they were their fathers, their sons, their brothers, or their kinsfolk. Allah has inscribed faith in their hearts and strengthened them with a spirit from Himself. He will admit them into Gardens beneath which rivers flow, to abide therein forever. Allah is pleased with them, and they are pleased with Him. They are the party of Allah; it is they who are the successful.\n\nThe expression \"Do you not see\" in verse 16 expresses astonishment and condemnation. Allah condemns the hypocrites for aiding and abetting a people with whom He is angry (the Jews), conveying the secrets of the believers to them, and knowingly taking false oaths. In the narrations concerning the occasion of revelation of this verse, it is recounted that our Prophet (peace be upon him) said to those with him, \"A man with the eyes of a devil will come to you now,\" and that a blue-eyed man arrived and swore an oath denying that he and his companions had cursed the Prophet. It is reported that the verse was revealed upon this incident.\n\nThe same theme is repeated in verse 17. It is emphasized that the hypocrites aided the Jews, that they belonged neither to the believers nor to the Jews, and that they wavered continuously between the two parties. Again within the context of the same narration, it is related that this verse was revealed upon the oaths taken by the hypocrite named Abdullah ibn Nabtal and his companions. There is no contradiction among the narrations; therefore, it can be said that the remainder of the surah was revealed on account of this event. Although there are differing views regarding the identity of Abdullah ibn Nabtal, there are narrations indicating that he was among the hypocrites or that he later repented.\n\nIn verse 18, the state of the hypocrites is likewise described with the same expressions, and the same narration is repeated once again. The repetitions in these verses aim to emphatically highlight the evil state of the hypocrites. The fact that the narrations come from different sources reinforces the authenticity of the incident.\n\nIn verse 19, it is stated that hypocrites possessing these characteristics are the party of Satan (hizb). Having been overtaken by Satan and having forgotten Allah, these people are Satan's partisans and will certainly suffer loss. For they have brought ruin upon their own souls by forfeiting eternal bliss and plunging themselves into punishment.\n\nIn verse 20, it is stated that those insolent ones who attempt to contend against Allah and His Messenger are counted, both in the past and the future, among the most wretched of people, those most deserving of humiliation. The greater the honor and might of the side they oppose, the more their own baseness increases in proportion.\n\nIn verse 21, it is stated that Allah decreed victory in pre-eternity in the Preserved Tablet (al-Lawh al-Mahfuz). According to Qatadah, this statement serves as an oath. Allah has decreed: \"Assuredly, I and My messengers shall prevail.\" Even if prophets are killed, they prevail in their cause through clear proof and the sword. For Allah is All-Powerful, has no equal or partner, aids His messengers, and can never be defeated.\n\nIn verse 22, it is emphasized that those who believe in Allah and the Last Day cannot love those who oppose Allah and His Messenger. Such love signifies affection for disbelief and is incompatible with faith. Believers do not love these enemies even if they are their fathers, their sons, their brothers, or their relatives. Allah has inscribed faith in the hearts of these believers and strengthened them with a spirit from Him. He will admit them into Gardens beneath which rivers flow; He will be pleased with them, and they will be pleased with Him. They are indeed the party of Allah, and it is only they who are the successful. The surah concludes in this manner, and Surah al-Hashr follows it."